        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Sub-Saharan Africa</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Tuareg</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a> from southwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> to Niger, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, with the Kinnin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>; the Ahaggar and Aïr mountains, the Adrar des Iforas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kidal">Kidal</a>, Agadez, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghat">Ghat</a>; an estimated 1.2 to 3 million people, about 2 million of them in Niger, the majority in Mali’s Kidal Region.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Tin Hinan, the founding queen, 4th–5th century; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> from the 7th century and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki school</a> from the 16th; the Sultanate of Agadez from 1449; confederations under an amenokal by the 19th century; French conquest from 1881 to 1920; the homeland divided among independent states in the 1960s; uprisings in the 1960s, from 1990 to 1996, from 2007 and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> War.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the tagelmust, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a> veil worn by men; the Tifinagh script; trans-Saharan salt caravans; confederations led by an amenokal; ranks of nobles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, clerics, artisans and enslaved people; matrilineal descent and the woman’s tent; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> jewellery and the Agadez Cross; the anzad violin, the tende drum and the guitar music of Tinariwen</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">accounts by Ibn Hawkal (10th century), El-Bekri, Edrisi, Ibn Battutah, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-africanus">Leo Africanus</a> and Ibn Khaldûn; the Tin Hinan tomb at Abalessa and its Tifinagh inscription; travellers such as James Richardson (1845–1846); <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-foucauld">Charles de Foucauld</a>’s dictionary and collections of poetry; anthropologists and linguists such as Susan Rasmussen, Jeffrey Heath and Karl-Gottfried Prasse; French colonial reports; genetic studies.</span></div>
