    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Tuareg</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahaggar-mountains.jpg" alt="Ahaggar Mountains" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Ahaggar Mountains <span class="kid-tale-cr">Mohammed Amri, CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>The Tuareg are an Amazigh people of the Sahara desert and the Sahel.</p><p>Their founding queen Tin Hinan is believed to have lived over 1,500 years ago, and lies buried in the Hoggar Mountains.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indigo.jpg" alt="Indigo" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Indigo <span class="kid-tale-cr">Photo by Evan Izer (Palladian), CC BY-SA 2.5</span></figcaption></figure><p>They call themselves Imuhagh, and also Kel Tagelmust, which means “veiled people”.</p><p>Tuareg men, not women, traditionally cover their faces with an indigo veil, the tagelmust, once they grow up.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/camel.jpg" alt="Camel" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Camel <span class="kid-tale-cr">Jjron, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>They live from their herds and from trade, and for centuries their camel caravans carried salt across the Sahara.</p><p>Their society was ranked, from nobles at the top down to enslaved people at the bottom.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/timbuktu.jpg" alt="Timbuktu" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Timbuktu <span class="kid-tale-cr">Flickr Emilio Labrador Santiago de Chile 3059349393 (Flickr), CC BY 2.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Most Tuareg are Muslims, and Tuareg clerics helped carry Islam across the Sahel.</p><p>Timbuktu, a city famed for its scholars, grew rich under the protection of the Tuareg.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moussa-ag-amastan.jpg" alt="Moussa ag Amastan" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Moussa ag Amastan <span class="kid-tale-cr">unknown; Public domainPublic domainfalsefalse This work is in the public domain in its country of origin and other countries and …, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>The Tuareg fought hard against the French, who conquered their lands between 1881 and 1920.</p><p>In the 1960s their homeland was split among five new countries, and many Tuareg have since risen up for it.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tuareg.jpg" alt="Tuareg" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Tuareg <span class="kid-tale-cr">Amine loua, CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Today more than a million Tuareg live in the Sahara and the Sahel, many of them in Niger.</p><p>Their smiths make silver jewellery such as the Agadez Cross, and festivals keep their music and dance alive.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Tuareg, who call themselves Imuhagh, Kel Tamasheq (“speakers of Tamasheq”) and Kel Tagelmust (“veiled people”), are an Amazigh people whose founding queen, Tin Hinan, is believed to have lived in the 4th or 5th century.</li><li>Organised in confederations under an amenokal and ranked into nobles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, clerics, artisans and enslaved people, they controlled trans-Saharan trade routes and ran salt caravans across the desert.</li><li>The Tuareg resisted French conquest from 1881 to 1920; after independence their homeland was divided among five states, and uprisings followed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> and Niger from the 1960s.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Among the Tuareg it is the men, not the women, who traditionally veil their faces: a man takes on the tagelmust when he reaches maturity, as a rite of passage to manhood.</p></aside>
    </section>

