    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">4th–5th century</span><span class="rf-text">Tin Hinan, the Tuareg founding queen, leads her people south into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a>; her tomb stands at Abalessa in the Hoggar</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">7th century</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> reaches <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>, and the Tuareg in time adopt it</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1449</span><span class="rf-text">A Tuareg ruling house founds the Sultanate of Agadez in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/air-mountains">Aïr Mountains</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1881</span><span class="rf-text">Tuareg fighters annihilate the French expedition led by Paul Flatters</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1902</span><span class="rf-text">The French defeat the Kel Ahaggar at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tit">Battle of Tit</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1990</span><span class="rf-text">After a clash at Tchin-Tabaraden, Tuareg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> and Niger rise and claim independence for their homeland</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1992–1995</span><span class="rf-text">Peace agreements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> and Niger decentralise power and bring Tuareg fighters into the national armies</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">April 2026</span><span class="rf-text">During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> War the Azawad Liberation Front captures <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kidal">Kidal</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao">Gao</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahaggar-mountains"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahaggar-mountains.jpg" alt="Ahaggar Mountains" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ahaggar-mountains">Ahaggar Mountains</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D8%A7%D8%B3%D9%83%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%85_2_-_%D8%AA%D9%85%D9%86%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%B3%D8%AA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mohammed Amri, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Tuareg are an Amazigh people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a>. They call themselves Imuhagh or Imushagh, a cognate of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> name Imazighen, and also Kel Tamasheq, “speakers of Tamasheq”, and Kel Tagelmust, “veiled people”; the name Amajagh, “freeman”, strictly meant the nobles alone. The name Tuareg probably comes from Targa, their name for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fezzan">Fezzan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>. They speak the Tuareg languages, Tamasheq, Tamahaq and Tamajeq, of the Berber branch of the Afroasiatic family, and write them in Tifinagh, the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libyco-berber-script">Libyco-Berber script</a>.</p><p>In antiquity the Tuareg moved south from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tafilalt">Tafilalt</a> into the Sahel under their founding queen Tin Hinan, who is believed to have lived in the 4th or 5th century; her monumental tomb at Abalessa in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahaggar-mountains">Hoggar Mountains</a> bears traces of a Tifinagh inscription. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garamantian-empire">Garamantes</a> of the Fezzan are often counted among their ancestors, and some researchers tie the rise of the Tuareg to the Garamantes’ fall; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamtuna">Lamtuna</a>, the core of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravid dynasty</a>, were close kin. Their oral tradition holds that the seven clans who moved south after the Arab invasions of the 11th century descend from the same mother.</p><p>Genetic studies describe North African Berber ancestry as a mosaic of local North African, Middle Eastern, European and sub-Saharan strands. Among the Tuareg the male lineage E-M81, the “Berber marker”, is the most common, and the Tuareg of the Fezzan carry maternal haplogroup H1 at the highest frequency yet found.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="society">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Society</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/camel"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/camel.jpg" alt="Camel" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/camel">Camel</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:07._Camel_Profile,_near_Silverton,_NSW,_07.07.2007.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jjron, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Tuareg are a pastoral people, living from livestock, trade and farming. By the 19th century their land was divided among confederations, sometimes called “drum groups” after the drum that marked the authority of their supreme chief, the amenokal, who ruled with a council of elders; among the major confederations were the Kel Ahaggar, Kel Ajjer, Kel Adagh and Kel Ayr. Each clan, or tawshet, was led by an amghar, an office handed down through the mother’s line, usually to the son of the chief’s sister.</p><p>Society was ranked. The nobles, imuhagh, held the monopoly of arms and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> and took tribute from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> herders, the imghad, who kept goats, sheep and donkeys and tended the nobles’ herds. The Ineslemen, the clerics, served as judges and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a>; artisan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a>, the inadan, made saddles, tools, weapons and jewellery; the farmers of the oases, the izeggaghan, kept a fifth of their harvest for themselves. At the bottom were the enslaved, the ikelan, taken in raids or bought in markets, whose status was inherited.</p><p>Tuareg culture is largely matrilineal. The tent belongs to the married woman and is first raised at her wedding, so that “making a tent” means to marry, and jewellery passes from mother to daughter. Patrilineal Islamic values, cousin marriage and polygyny are thought to have been laid over this older order.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Worldview</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/timbuktu.jpg" alt="Timbuktu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Donkeys,_Timbuktu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Flickr Emilio Labrador Santiago de Chile 3059349393 (Flickr), CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> the Tuareg held to traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> religion, and parts of its cosmology and ritual live on, especially among women, in allusions to matrilineal spirits, fertility, the earth and ancestresses, and in a widespread “cult of the dead”, a form of ancestor veneration. The men’s veil is said to come from the belief that it wards off evil spirits, and amulets holding sacred objects and, more recently, verses of the Qur’an are widely worn.</p><p>Islam reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> in the 7th century. The Tuareg at first resisted it in their strongholds; in the 16th century, under the guidance of El Maghili, they embraced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki school</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a>, which most follow today. The clerical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> of the Ineslemen spread the faith across North Africa and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a>, and the Tuareg helped carry it into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sudan">Western Sudan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>, a centre famed for its scholars, is said to have been founded by Tuareg at the start of the 12th century and flourished under a Tuareg confederation’s protection, though modern scholars find too little evidence to name its founders.</p><p>In 1449 a Tuareg ruling house founded the Sultanate of Agadez in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/air-mountains">Aïr Mountains</a>. In the 18th century the Tuareg scholar Jibril ibn ’Umar preached revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a>, and his student <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a> went on to lead the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a> jihads and found the Sokoto Caliphate.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="arts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Arts &amp; Knowledge</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-foucauld"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-de-foucauld.jpg" alt="Charles de Foucauld" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-foucauld">Charles de Foucauld</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_de_Foucauld.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Edsdet, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Tuareg metalwork is famous. Smiths of the inadan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a> make jewellery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, coloured glass and iron, worn by men as well as women, among them the tanaghilt or Agadez Cross, cast by the lost-wax method and now a symbol of Tuareg culture and political rights, the takoba sword, necklaces, earrings and decorated pilgrimage boxes. Silver, much of it from old Austrian coins and five-franc pieces, is thought to bring happiness, while gold is thought to bring misfortune. Saddles, leatherwork and tents covered with skins or mats complete the craft.</p><p>Poetry, often elegiac, epigrammatic or amatory, is composed in great quantity; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-foucauld">Charles de Foucauld</a>, who compiled perhaps the earliest Tuareg dictionary, and other ethnographers preserved thousands of poems. Traditional music rests on the anzad, a one-stringed violin played at night gatherings, and the tende, a goatskin drum played at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> races and festivities, with songs such as asak and tisiway sung by women and men.</p><p>In the 1980s rebel fighters founded Tinariwen, joining electric guitars to Tuareg styles; their music spread through areas cut off by the rebellion, and they toured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 2004, followed by Bombino, Mdou Moctar and others. The night sky has Tuareg names too: Amanar, Orion, is “the warrior of the desert”.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="exchange">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Contact &amp; Exchange</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/moussa-ag-amastan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moussa-ag-amastan.jpg" alt="Moussa ag Amastan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/moussa-ag-amastan">Moussa ag Amastan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MousaAgAmastane-Paris.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">unknown; Public domainPublic domainfalsefalse This work is in the public domain in its country of origin and other countries and …, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Since prehistoric times the Tuareg have organised caravans across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>, first with oxen and horses, later with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>, carrying salt from mines such as Taoudenni, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilma">Bilma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fachi">Fachi</a>: the Azalay ran from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> to Taoudenni, the Taghlamt from Agadez to Bilma. Arab writers from Ibn Hawkal in the 10th century to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-africanus">Leo Africanus</a> in the 16th described them as the Mulatthamin, “the veiled ones”. Raids, rezzous, on other confederations and on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaamba">Chaamba</a>, Toubou, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> brought camels, goods and captives, who were kept as slaves or sold.</p><p>The Tuareg resisted French conquest. They annihilated Paul Flatters’ expedition in 1881; in 1902 the French defeated the Kel Ahaggar at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tit">Battle of Tit</a>, after which some moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> and the rest submitted under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moussa-ag-amastan">Moussa ag Amastan</a>, while Kel Ajjer under Sheikh Amoud Ag El Mokhtar, backed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and the Senusiyya, fought on from 1913 to 1920. After numerous massacres by the French and the southern Tuareg, treaties were forced on them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> in 1905 and Niger in 1917.</p><p>French rule leaned on the nobles and did not free the enslaved: after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> some 50,000 Bella were still held by Tuareg masters in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao">Gao</a>–Timbuktu area alone, and mass desertions began in 1946.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kidal"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kidal.jpg" alt="Kidal" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kidal">Kidal</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maison_artisans.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The original uploader was Taguelmoust at French Wikipedia., CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Independence in the 1960s divided the Tuareg lands among Niger, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a>. Restrictions on nomadic life and desertification drove some to farming or to the towns. The Malian army put down an uprising in the Adrar des Iforas in the 1960s; in 1990, after a clash at Tchin-Tabaraden in Niger, Tuareg in both countries claimed independence for their homeland, and fighting under leaders such as Mano Dayak killed thousands before agreements in 1992 and 1995. Violence rose again in Niger in 2007, and in April 2026, during the Mali War, the Azawad Liberation Front captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kidal">Kidal</a>.</p><p>Old hierarchies have lasted: Niger outlawed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> only in 2003, and some Tuareg communities still hold slaves. In northern Niger, uranium mining by the French firm Areva has degraded Tuareg grazing land and uses up scarce water, and the Tuareg there remain poor and unrepresented in government. In 2021 militants of IS-GS killed 141 people, most of them Tuareg, in villages around Tillia.</p><p>A Tuareg revival grew with Berberism in the 1990s, and three Tuareg flags have been designed since 1998. Festivals such as the Festival in the Desert near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> gather Tuareg music and dance, and the exhibition “Art of Being Tuareg” opened at Stanford in 2007.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Tuareg are an Amazigh people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a> who trace their origins to the founding queen Tin Hinan and became Muslims and carriers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Organised in ranked confederations, they ran the salt caravans across the desert and resisted French conquest until 1920; divided among five states since the 1960s, they have risen repeatedly for their homeland.</em></p>

